---
import brand from '@arcvia/brand'
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro'
import plans from '@arcvia/brand/plans'
---

<Legal
  title="Terms and conditions"
  description={`The terms you agree to when using ${brand.name}.`}
  updated="2026-08-18"
>
  <p>
    These terms govern your use of {brand.name}. By creating an account you
    accept them.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The service</h2>
  <p>
    {brand.name} converts architectural drawings and 3D models into interactive
    walkthroughs, still renders and bills of quantities, and hosts the published
    result at a public URL.
  </p>
  {
    !plans.billingEnabled && (
      <p>
        <strong>The service is currently free.</strong> No payment is taken, no
        card is stored, and no feature is withheld pending payment. If that
        changes we will give notice before any charge is made, and you will
        never be charged for a period you have already used for free.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>2. Your account</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You are responsible for what happens under your credentials.</li>
    <li>
      One person per account. Team members get their own seats rather than
      sharing a login — shared logins are the usual cause of a lost project.
    </li>
    <li>Tell us promptly if you believe an account has been compromised.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Your content, and who owns it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>You own everything you upload and everything you produce here.</strong>
    Drawings, models, textures, scenes, renders and published walkthroughs remain
    yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    You grant us only the licence required to actually run the service: to
    store your files, process them on render workers, and serve the walkthroughs
    you have chosen to publish. That licence ends when you delete the content.
  </p>
  <p>You confirm that you have the right to upload what you upload.</p>

  <h2>4. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>You may not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Upload material you do not have the rights to.</li>
    <li>
      Use the render pipeline for workloads unrelated to visualising your own
      projects — it is a shared GPU resource with real cost.
    </li>
    <li>
      Attempt to circumvent credit metering, rate limits or access controls.
    </li>
    <li>Resell access to the platform itself without a written agreement.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Publishing a walkthrough for a client, embedding it on your own site, and
    charging your client for the work is expressly fine — that is what the
    product is for.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Credits and rendering</h2>
  <p>
    Renders and lightmap bakes consume credits, which exist to bound GPU
    spending rather than to sell you anything. Credits are charged when a job is
    <em>queued</em>, not when it completes. Cancelling a job that has not started
    refunds the credits; cancelling one that is already rendering does not,
    because the compute has already been spent.
  </p>
  <p>A job that fails through our fault is refunded in full.</p>

  <h2>6. Availability</h2>
  <p>
    We aim for the service to be available continuously but do not guarantee it.
    Render throughput in particular is capped, and jobs may queue at busy times.
    We will give notice of planned maintenance where we reasonably can.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Ending the agreement</h2>
  <p>
    You may close your account at any time. We may suspend an account that
    breaches section 4, and will tell you why. On closure you can export your
    models and renders; after 30 days the stored files are deleted permanently.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided as-is. We are not liable for indirect or
    consequential loss, including lost profit or lost business arising from a
    project decision made on the basis of a visualisation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A visualisation is not a construction document.</strong> Dimensions,
    quantities and bills of materials produced here are derived from what you
    supplied and are for design and presentation purposes. Verify them against
    the issued drawings before building anything or pricing a contract.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    We may update these terms. Material changes will be notified in the product
    before they take effect.
  </p>
</Legal>
